\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The missing relation between frames and cell directions}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{The cubical maps #{u_i\wedge} and #{u_i\cdot} in
the [rotation-only operator](fdip-00YO) use the fixed cell directions. The internal
exterior action changes coefficients in a separate factor.
No field relating these two sorts of directions, with a compatible
affine gauge law, has been declared. Such a relation is often
called frame or soldering data.}

\p{For the operators actually defined here, the conclusions are
exact: the edge-direction proposal fails linear conjugacy in
\ref{fdip-00YZ}, and the rotation-only operator is independent
of every translation label in \ref{fdip-00Z2}, even when torsion
classes differ. Whether a separately defined affine-sensitive
matter operator has a torsion-sensitive determinant remains open.
Neither conclusion gives a general obstruction to affine matter,
a frame dynamics, or a preferred spinor representation.}
